{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And YHWH your Elohim turned for you the curse into a blessing.</b> This expression needs explanation, for how is it possible to say that the curse itself would become a blessing? Even though blessings of the same nature as the things he wished to curse them with came in place of the curses, nonetheless the curse itself is not a blessing. It is also difficult regarding what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 105a): from the blessings of that wicked man you learn what was in his heart to curse them, etc., and they conclude: all of them reverted to curse except for synagogues, etc., as it says: ״וַיַּהֲפֹךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לְךָ אֶת הַקְּלָלָה״, ״curses״ is not written, etc. — meaning only one of them was turned into a blessing. And it is difficult: what makes this blessing different from the rest of them? And furthermore, what in the language of the verse indicates that specifically synagogues reverted to blessing?" "And the explanation for all this is that Balaam was a sorcerer, and he would see in the astrology of Israel that it pointed to all these things that he sought to curse them with — that they would not have synagogues, etc.; that their kingdom would not endure, etc. And it is known that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not disrupt the astrological configuration for the sake of Israel's merit except in a place where there is no way at all to redirect the force of the configuration toward good; but in a place where it is possible to uphold the decree of the configuration and to resolve it by a different path, then the Holy One, blessed be He, does not disrupt it. For example, the star whose name is ״רָעָה״ (Ra'ah, 'Evil'), which was rising to meet Israel and portended blood — the Holy One, blessed be He, turned it into the blood of circumcision. And as our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 156a): this one born under the planet Mars will be a shedder of blood, etc., as explained above in parashat Bo (comment on Exodus 10). And all the curses enumerated there in the Gemara — the Shechinah will not rest upon them; their kingdom will not endure; they will have no olive trees and vineyards, etc. — in all of these there was no way to interpret them for good in order to uphold the decree of the configuration while Israel would suffer no harm. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to disrupt the configuration from indicating these things entirely. Consequently, all of them reverted to curse when Israel sinned, and they had no merit powerful enough to stand against the configuration." "But regarding the indication that they would not have synagogues and study halls, the Holy One, blessed be He, did not need to disrupt the configuration, for there was room to uphold the decree of the configuration — that they would not have synagogues — but for their own good. And that is along the lines of what Rashi explained: ״מִשְׁכְּנוֹתֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — they are held as pledges for their sin, as it says (Lamentations 4:11): ״כִּלָּה ה׳ חֲמָתוֹ וַיַּצֶּת אֵשׁ בְּצִיּוֹן״, and this is certainly a great good — that HaShem would exhaust His wrath on wood and stones and we would be spared, for one who has a pledge to give for his debt, they do not collect from his very life. And that is what is meant by what it says: ״וַיַּהֲפֹךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לְךָ אֶת הַקְּלָלָה לִבְרָכָה״ — for the very wording of the curse itself, which is that they would not have synagogues, was turned into a blessing, because through their not having synagogues their lives would be spared. And that is what Balaam said: ״מַה טֹּבוּ אֹהָלֶיךָ יַעֲקֹב מִשְׁכְּנֹתֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — meaning, your good is that they are your מִשְׁכְּנֹתֶיךָ, held as pledges against you, and you are spared. And therefore this matter did not revert to curse, for the decree of the configuration was upheld and was not disrupted. Consequently, it is not applicable to say that the indication of the configuration would revert to its former state when Israel is unworthy."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:34.998916118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}